1
00:00:00,110 --> 00:00:05,010
In Pennsylvania, there is a nuclear reactor that partially melted down in 1979.

2
00:00:05,710 --> 00:00:08,790
Regulators spent decades making sure it would never run again.

3
00:00:10,150 --> 00:00:12,430
This year, it is scheduled to switch back on,

4
00:00:12,590 --> 00:00:15,770
not to power homes, to power artificial intelligence.

5
00:00:17,170 --> 00:00:21,290
The federal investigation into why it failed the first time found something specific.

6
00:00:21,750 --> 00:00:23,890
Not a broken part. A mindset.

7
00:00:24,550 --> 00:00:27,870
A habit of trusting the equipment and ignoring the people in the room.

8
00:00:27,870 --> 00:00:32,940
That finding is a real, public federal report.

9
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:38,600
And right now, a regulator is deciding whether the exact conditions it warned about have

10
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:42,080
actually been fixed. This is that report,

11
00:00:42,480 --> 00:00:44,520
held up against the paperwork being filed today,

12
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:48,600
beat by beat. You're watching Black Box Breakdown.

13
00:00:50,020 --> 00:00:52,260
The meltdown reactor A are just reopened.

14
00:00:53,880 --> 00:00:56,500
Chapter 1. The morning nobody was ready for.

15
00:00:57,600 --> 00:01:01,280
March 28, 1979. Four in the morning.

16
00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:03,460
Three Mile Island, Pennsylvania.

17
00:01:04,280 --> 00:01:08,440
Control room operator Craig Faust is at the panel of Unit 2 when the first alarm cuts

18
00:01:08,440 --> 00:01:12,220
through the dark. A maintenance job on a water filter,

19
00:01:12,500 --> 00:01:16,160
three floors away, trips the pumps feeding the plant's steam generators.

20
00:01:16,900 --> 00:01:20,080
Within seconds, the reactor shuts itself down automatically,

21
00:01:20,460 --> 00:01:25,640
exactly as designed. Then a relief valve at the top of the reactor's pressurizer

22
00:01:25,640 --> 00:01:27,120
opens to bleed off pressure,

23
00:01:27,440 --> 00:01:31,180
exactly as designed. And then it does something it was not designed to do.

24
00:01:31,380 --> 00:01:35,200
It sticks open. In the control room,

25
00:01:35,480 --> 00:01:37,800
a light on the panel shows the valve is closed.

26
00:01:38,300 --> 00:01:40,820
But that light was never wired to the valve itself.

27
00:01:41,560 --> 00:01:44,220
It was wired to the signal telling the valve to close.

28
00:01:44,940 --> 00:01:46,500
Nobody in that room knew the difference.

29
00:01:47,560 --> 00:01:52,060
For two hours, coolant drains out of an open valve that every instrument in the room

30
00:01:52,060 --> 00:01:56,440
insists is shut. This exact failure had happened before.

31
00:01:56,940 --> 00:02:00,200
18 months earlier, at a nearly identical reactor in Ohio,

32
00:02:00,680 --> 00:02:02,540
a relief valve stuck open there too.

33
00:02:03,180 --> 00:02:04,520
Federal regulators studied it.

34
00:02:04,820 --> 00:02:07,000
Nobody warned the operators at Three Mile Island.

35
00:02:09,180 --> 00:02:12,800
Craig Faust and the other operators on shift that morning did not know any of this.

36
00:02:13,220 --> 00:02:17,020
They were reading a control panel that was built to hide the one thing they most needed

37
00:02:17,020 --> 00:02:21,280
to see. Chapter two, the mistake that made it worse.

38
00:02:22,940 --> 00:02:25,480
Operators are trained to watch one gauge above all others,

39
00:02:25,800 --> 00:02:27,300
the water level in the pressurizer.

40
00:02:27,820 --> 00:02:30,000
As coolant drains out through the stuck valve,

41
00:02:30,300 --> 00:02:32,640
that gauge reads full, rising,

42
00:02:32,940 --> 00:02:37,940
even. So operator Edward Frederick does exactly what his training tells him to

43
00:02:37,940 --> 00:02:40,020
do when a reactor looks like it's overfilling.

44
00:02:40,380 --> 00:02:42,820
He throttles back the emergency cooling pumps.

45
00:02:44,500 --> 00:02:49,280
It is the single decision that turns a manageable equipment failure into a partial meltdown.

46
00:02:49,860 --> 00:02:51,060
The core starts to uncover,

47
00:02:51,600 --> 00:02:53,420
and no one in the room can see it happening.

48
00:02:54,880 --> 00:02:57,760
Eight minutes in, operator Craig Faust finds something else.

49
00:02:58,580 --> 00:03:02,620
Emergency feed water valves left closed after maintenance that were never supposed to

50
00:03:02,620 --> 00:03:04,420
be closed at all. He reopens them.

51
00:03:04,660 --> 00:03:06,720
It is the first thing anyone gets right that morning.

52
00:03:08,180 --> 00:03:10,980
By then, more than a hundred alarms are sounding at once.

53
00:03:10,980 --> 00:03:15,880
The control panel physically cannot show the operators everything failing in time for

54
00:03:15,880 --> 00:03:21,080
them to react to it. A hydrogen bubble forms at the top of the reactor vessel.

55
00:03:21,500 --> 00:03:24,320
For days, nobody is certain whether it can explode.

56
00:03:25,100 --> 00:03:28,500
Radioactive gas is deliberately vented from the plant to relieve pressure.

57
00:03:29,860 --> 00:03:33,780
Pennsylvania's governor advises pregnant women and small children within five miles to

58
00:03:33,780 --> 00:03:38,280
leave. An estimated 140 ,000 people evacuate the region on their own.

59
00:03:39,300 --> 00:03:41,600
Four days later, President Carter,

60
00:03:41,980 --> 00:03:43,820
a trained nuclear engineer himself,

61
00:03:44,200 --> 00:03:48,740
walks into the Unit 2 control room in person to prove to the country it was safe enough

62
00:03:48,740 --> 00:03:53,960
to stand in. Chapter 3. The report they didn't want to hear.

63
00:03:55,120 --> 00:03:59,840
Seven months later, a federal commission led by mathematician John Kemeny delivers its

64
00:03:59,840 --> 00:04:02,680
verdict. And it is not the verdict the industry expected.

65
00:04:03,760 --> 00:04:08,420
The commission's central finding was not about the stuck valve or the misleading light

66
00:04:08,420 --> 00:04:11,360
or even the training. It was about a mindset,

67
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:16,320
an industry so focused on the safety of its equipment that it downplayed the one thing

68
00:04:16,320 --> 00:04:17,520
actually running the plant,

69
00:04:17,940 --> 00:04:23,240
the people in the room. The report singled out the regulator itself.

70
00:04:23,800 --> 00:04:25,200
The Nuclear Regulatory Commission,

71
00:04:25,560 --> 00:04:30,460
it found, was so narrowly focused on whether individual parts met code that it never

72
00:04:30,460 --> 00:04:34,760
stepped back to ask whether the plant as a whole was actually being run safely.

73
00:04:36,220 --> 00:04:37,820
It recommended sweeping changes,

74
00:04:38,240 --> 00:04:39,280
better operator training,

75
00:04:39,760 --> 00:04:44,420
redesigned control rooms and a federal inspector living on site at every reactor in the

76
00:04:44,420 --> 00:04:48,720
country permanently. The health toll is still disputed.

77
00:04:49,280 --> 00:04:53,320
One 1997 university study found higher cancer rates downwind of the plant.

78
00:04:54,140 --> 00:04:56,620
Federal regulators and other researchers disagree,

79
00:04:56,840 --> 00:04:59,040
pointing to smoking rates and study design.

80
00:04:59,040 --> 00:05:02,840
Nearly 50 years later, there is still no settled answer.

81
00:05:04,780 --> 00:05:07,340
What is not disputed is what happened to the reactor itself.

82
00:05:08,280 --> 00:05:12,920
Unit 2 never ran again. Its cleanup took 14 years and cost roughly a billion dollars.

83
00:05:13,360 --> 00:05:15,240
It was, everyone agreed, finished.

84
00:05:17,540 --> 00:05:19,980
Chapter 4. The switch nobody expected.

85
00:05:21,400 --> 00:05:23,400
Not Unit 2. Its neighbour,

86
00:05:23,880 --> 00:05:29,160
Unit 1, kept running for decades after until it was shut down for purely economic reasons

87
00:05:29,160 --> 00:05:32,320
in 2019. It sat idle for five years.

88
00:05:33,840 --> 00:05:39,000
Then, in September 2024, Constellation Energy announced it would spend $1 .6

89
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:41,660
billion to bring that idle reactor back online.

90
00:05:42,320 --> 00:05:44,460
Not for the power grid. For one customer,

91
00:05:45,360 --> 00:05:50,650
Microsoft. The deal is a 20 -year contract to sell the plant's entire

92
00:05:50,650 --> 00:05:56,150
output, 835 MW, to power the data centres running artificial intelligence.

93
00:05:57,410 --> 00:05:59,530
The plant even has a new name now,

94
00:06:00,030 --> 00:06:01,290
Crane Clean Energy Centre.

95
00:06:01,830 --> 00:06:04,150
The original target for switching back on was 2028.

96
00:06:05,170 --> 00:06:06,810
It has since been moved a year earlier.

97
00:06:08,170 --> 00:06:12,450
Three Mile Island Alert, the same watchdog group that has monitored this site since before

98
00:06:12,450 --> 00:06:14,510
the meltdown, calls the process rushed.

99
00:06:15,070 --> 00:06:18,670
Its director says the fuel is already being delivered before regulators have even finished

100
00:06:18,670 --> 00:06:20,110
reviewing it, calling it,

101
00:06:20,370 --> 00:06:22,610
in his words, quote, a farce.

102
00:06:23,510 --> 00:06:28,390
Chapter 5, The Paperwork In June 2026,

103
00:06:28,870 --> 00:06:32,490
the Nuclear Regulatory Commission released its draft assessment for reauthorising the

104
00:06:32,490 --> 00:06:34,330
plant. Its preliminary finding,

105
00:06:34,790 --> 00:06:36,570
no significant environmental impact.

106
00:06:38,070 --> 00:06:40,010
The public comment period closed in July.

107
00:06:40,650 --> 00:06:44,110
Fresh nuclear fuel is scheduled to arrive on site by the end of this year,

108
00:06:44,370 --> 00:06:48,990
sitting in storage, waiting on a final licence decision expected as early as next spring.

109
00:06:50,350 --> 00:06:54,050
Separately, the region's power grid operator has had to intervene,

110
00:06:54,650 --> 00:06:58,890
granting a special waiver just to make room on the grid for this one reactor's return.

111
00:07:00,370 --> 00:07:03,630
As of now, the final environmental review has not been published.

112
00:07:04,010 --> 00:07:05,610
It is expected within weeks.

113
00:07:06,090 --> 00:07:08,990
The restart of a reactor that partially melted down is,

114
00:07:09,270 --> 00:07:13,690
right now, an open fire. Chapter 6,

115
00:07:14,110 --> 00:07:19,110
Putting the Old Report on Trial So here is the question nobody at a press conference

116
00:07:19,110 --> 00:07:20,890
is being asked directly. Not,

117
00:07:21,330 --> 00:07:23,330
is nuclear power safe? This one.

118
00:07:23,910 --> 00:07:28,750
Has the specific mindset the Kemeny Commission blamed in 1979 actually been fixed on

119
00:07:28,750 --> 00:07:30,650
this reactor before it runs again?

120
00:07:32,730 --> 00:07:36,770
The Commission's first finding was a control room built to hide critical information

121
00:07:36,770 --> 00:07:39,670
from operators. Modern reactor control rooms,

122
00:07:39,970 --> 00:07:44,390
including this one's, have been redesigned since with clearer instrumentation.

123
00:07:44,390 --> 00:07:47,130
On paper, that finding has been addressed.

124
00:07:48,710 --> 00:07:53,330
The Commission's second finding was a regulator too narrowly focused on individual parts

125
00:07:53,330 --> 00:07:54,470
to see the bigger picture.

126
00:07:54,890 --> 00:07:59,830
That is precisely the criticism Three Mile Island Alert is making again today about the

127
00:07:59,830 --> 00:08:01,970
same regulator on the same site.

128
00:08:03,270 --> 00:08:07,030
The Commission's third finding was an industry moving faster than its own oversight could

129
00:08:07,030 --> 00:08:09,850
keep up with. A reactor idle for five years,

130
00:08:10,130 --> 00:08:12,470
restarting on a contract signed for a technology,

131
00:08:12,470 --> 00:08:16,610
artificial intelligence, that barely existed the last time this plant ran.

132
00:08:17,970 --> 00:08:22,150
Nobody involved is claiming this reactor is the same one that failed in 1979.

133
00:08:22,870 --> 00:08:25,010
It has new fuel, new inspections,

134
00:08:25,550 --> 00:08:28,150
decades of accumulated regulation behind it.

135
00:08:28,510 --> 00:08:32,230
But the Commission's real warning was never about one broken valve.

136
00:08:33,570 --> 00:08:38,650
It was about how easy it is to trust a system right up until the moment the system

137
00:08:38,650 --> 00:08:43,770
is wrong. That is the exact test this restart is running again live this

138
00:08:43,770 --> 00:08:47,970
year. What stays with me here isn't the meltdown.

139
00:08:48,630 --> 00:08:50,190
Nobody died from it directly,

140
00:08:50,210 --> 00:08:53,590
and the plant survived to be studied in exhaustive detail.

141
00:08:55,050 --> 00:08:57,550
What stays with me is that the fix it produced,

142
00:08:57,870 --> 00:09:01,170
a federal report about paying attention to people instead of just parts,

143
00:09:01,450 --> 00:09:06,550
is being tested again on the exact same site decades later for a technology nobody

144
00:09:06,550 --> 00:09:08,370
in that control room could have imagined.

145
00:09:09,510 --> 00:09:12,110
The paperwork on this restart is still open.

146
00:09:12,530 --> 00:09:13,770
By the time you're watching this,

147
00:09:13,950 --> 00:09:15,470
some of it may already be decided.

148
00:09:17,570 --> 00:09:20,530
If a warning that got ignored for 18 months unsettled you,

149
00:09:20,790 --> 00:09:22,490
wait until you see the repair shortcut.

150
00:09:22,870 --> 00:09:27,650
Boeing made seven years before it killed 520 people on Japan Airlines Flight 123.

